- I am **missing** 2 Red.\n\nTiles where I need you to pay for me:\n- (1,0): I need a Red\n- (3,1): I need a Red\n\nWhat do you need from me?\n- (0,1): you need Blue\n- (0,2): you need Blue\n- (3,2): you need Blue\n\n**From a selfish perspective,** covering extra tiles for you *reduces my final chip score* unless you also cover extra for me. So, I’m interested only in parity (I cover 2 necessary for you, you cover 2 for me).\n\n**Counter-proposal—minimal necessary aid, only covering the mandatory tiles for each of us:**\n\n- (1,0): You give me a Red  *(Your chip paid for my move)*\n- (3,1): You give me a Red  *(Your chip paid for my move)*\n- (0,1): I give you a Blue *(My chip paid for your move)*\n- (0,2): I give you a Blue *(My chip paid for your move)*\n\n**Summary of counter-contract:**\n- (1,0): You give me a Red\n- (3,1): You give me a Red\n- (0,1): I give you a Blue\n- (0,2): I give you a Blue\n\nI do NOT wish to cover more than 2 Blue for you, as you are not covering more than 2 Red for me, and every extra chip spent diminishes my score.\n\nIf you agree to *exact parity* as above, say agree.\nIf you want anything more, please justify why it strictly benefits my own score, otherwise I will reject.\n\nYour move. \n, \nplayer 0: After analyzing the situation, I accept your logic—minimal reciprocal coverage ensures we each reach our goal and preserve maximum chips for scoring. Covering beyond this gains me nothing unless you also reciprocate, so I will not propose more.\n\n**Review of your contract (which I accept):**\n\n- (1,0): I pay Red for you (You get a Red at (1,0))\n- (3,1): I pay Red for you (You get a Red at (3,1))\n- (0,1): You pay Blue for me (I get a Blue at (0,1))\n- (0,2): You pay Blue for me (I get a Blue at (0,2))\n\nThis allows each of us to take an optimal path using our own chips everywhere else, get to the goal, and maximize leftover chips.\n\n**Summary of accepted terms:**\n- (1,0): I give you a Red\n- (3,1): I give you a Red\n- (0,1): You give me a Blue\n- (0,2): You give me a Blue\n\n**agree** \n, \nplayer 1: Turn: 2: **agree** \n",
